Classification systems
  Anklyze maps answers to four published classifications. They belong to their
  authors; Anklyze is not affiliated with or endorsed by them.

  Lauge-Hansen N. Fractures of the ankle. II. Combined experimental-surgical and
    experimental-roentgenologic investigations. Arch Surg. 1950;60(5):957-985.
  Danis R. Les fractures malléolaires. In: Théorie et pratique de
    l'ostéosynthèse. Paris: Masson; 1949.
  Weber BG. Die Verletzungen des oberen Sprunggelenkes. Bern: Huber; 1966.
  Meinberg EG, Agel J, Roberts CS, Karam MD, Kellam JF. Fracture and Dislocation
    Classification Compendium-2018. J Orthop Trauma. 2018;32 Suppl 1:S1-S170.
    (AO/OTA classification, AO Foundation and Orthopaedic Trauma Association.)
  Bartoníček J, Rammelt S, Kostlivý K, Vaněček V, Klika D, Trešl I. Anatomy and
    classification of the posterior tibial fragment in ankle fractures.
    Arch Orthop Trauma Surg. 2015;135(4):505-516.

Third-party content
  Landing page ankle renders (frontend/src/assets/landing/ankle*.webp, used by
  frontend/src/components/AnklePlate.tsx):
    BodyParts3D, © The Database Center for Life Science, https://lifesciencedb.jp/bp3d/
    Source files (BodyParts3D 3.0): FMA24477.obj, FMA24480.obj and FMA24482.obj
    (right tibia, fibula and talus), licensed under CC BY-SA 2.1 Japan in the
    files; the database licence page now grants CC BY 4.0,
    https://dbarchive.biosciencedbc.jp/en/bodyparts3d/lic.html
    Changes: the same adaptation as the classifier's 3D ankle model (tibia and
    fibula cut 100 mm above the joint, holes and cuts closed, meshes refined by one
    Loop subdivision step, ambient occlusion baked, malleolus regions tagged),
    rendered once as still images in anterolateral view (yaw 35 degrees, pitch
    8 degrees), cropped to the joint and the distal 44 mm of the tibia and
    fibula, with a faint rule round the silhouette; the second image tints the
    distal fibular fragment up to a drawn Weber B fracture line. The labels, the
    syndesmosis bracket and the fracture line are drawn over the images by
    AnklePlate.tsx.
    The images are licensed under CC BY-SA 4.0,
    https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/ ; this does not extend to the
    Anklyze code.

  Frontal ankle drawing (frontend/src/components/anatomy/frontBones.ts, used by
  the classifier's AnatomyView.tsx):
    Adapted from "Weber Classification - latin.svg" by DrFO.Tn, Wikimedia Commons,
    https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Weber_Classification_-_latin.svg
    Licensed under CC BY 4.0, https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/
    Changes: only the Weber B panel is used; colour fills, gradients, cartilage,
    ligaments, texture marks, the lower calcaneus, the arrow and the label were
    removed; bone outlines were redrawn as line art; two outline details hidden
    under ligaments in the original were smoothed; the drawing is cropped.
    For the classifier, the two fibular pieces are joined into one intact fibula
    outline with the fracture edges left out.

  Lateral ankle drawing (frontend/src/components/anatomy/lateralBones.ts, used by
  the classifier's AnatomyView.tsx):
    Adapted from "Ankle.svg", Wikimedia Commons,
    https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ankle.svg
    An SVG version by Jak of a drawing by the U.S. federal government
    (pueblo.gsa.gov); public domain in the United States (PD-USGov). No
    attribution is required; it is credited as a courtesy.
    Changes: only the tibia, fibula and talus outlines are used; ligaments,
    tendon, foot bones and labels were removed; the distal tibial articular
    surface with the posterior lip, the talar dome and the posterior talus,
    hidden under ligaments and the fibula in the original, were completed as
    simple curves, the dome parallel to the plafond; the lateral malleolus tip
    was joined into one contour; the drawing is cropped.
